# IO attack, session 3: does pause make DMA reads safe (Z), does the stock KERNAL matter (T, S), does a bus-idle gate make polling safe (G, H)? Fixed order.
# Same stopping rules as session 1/2 (session.py): an arm at 5 failures stops, the poll arms Z, S, G, H at 4;
# 3 wedges in a row needing hands stop the block; `stop` at any prompt or Ctrl-C ends the session.
# All blocks use the Breadbin disk. Blocks 2 (T, S) switch the Ultimate's Kernal ROM to the stock one (discovered at
# run time; the run refuses if it cannot tell) and the next non-stock arm puts it back (reboot each way); the restore
# trap and read-back cover the end. Blocks 1, 3 and 4 run the machine's normal (JiffyDOS) KERNAL.
1  DZ 8     # root mount: D clean vs Z (machine:pause, 1000-byte screen read, machine:resume every 0.5 s from the start)
2  TS 8     # KERNAL load of the boot file, STOCK KERNAL: T clean (no reads) vs S (1000 B every 0.5 s from the typed load)
3  DG 8     # root mount: D clean vs G (Q, but each screen read gated on three $DD00 reads showing bus idle)
4  KH 8     # KERNAL load of the boot file: K clean vs H (J, gated the same way)
